import SwiftUI
import KwipMLCore

/// Ordinary Train authoring practice; historical scored recipe receipts remain readable.
enum TrainDataAccessLessonContent {
    static let version = 5
    static let title = "Choose training observations and batches"
    static let isAssessed = false
    static let objective = "Map prepared table columns and configure data access and batching directly on Train while keeping the prepared training source and held-out validation rows separate."
    static let terms = "**Observation:** one input with its aligned desired outcome and row identity. **Data access:** how Train visits the already prepared training observations. **Batch:** a group processed together during one adjustment. **Collation:** the rule that combines rows into a batch. **Seed:** a number that makes a random ordering repeatable. **Prefetch:** upcoming groups prepared ahead by each separate reader process."
    static let steps = [
        "Open Train data access practice. Its existing Table Input, Partition and Preprocess flow prepares the observations. Connect Preprocess’s Training and Validation table outputs directly to Train’s corresponding inputs, alongside Model definition. Select Train and open Data mapping in Configuration. Keep signal then context as input columns and label as target; both populations use that exact order. Then open Data access and batches. These settings act on the prepared training partition; they do not replace Table Input, read another file or define the model's input shape.",
        "Keep Indexed rows and the configured batch size of 7. Run the CPU example under its three-pass budget. The bundled 64 observations split into 32 training, 16 validation and 16 protected test rows. Each training pass therefore has four full groups of 7 and one final group of 4. Keep Drop incomplete final batch off so every selected training row is used. Select Train → Metrics while it runs: Training loss reports completed groups before the epoch finishes. Validation appears after its separate evaluation, not after every group.",
        "Turn Drop incomplete final batch on, apply and run again. For this example each pass now uses four groups of 7, leaving four selected training observations unused in that pass. This is a deliberate batching policy, not a source-read failure. Restore the switch before continuing.",
        "Choose Selected training positions and enter 4, 1, 0 in that order. Positions start at zero in the original prepared training partition. Inputs, targets and row identities stay together. This chooses three training observations; it does not re-split or reduce the separate validation partition. Restore Indexed rows afterward.",
        "Compare Finite training prefix with Maximum training rows set to 5 and row mixing off. It visits the first five prepared training observations and restarts from the same finite snapshot each pass. This is not an external streaming source or an out-of-memory reader: the prepared tensors are already materialized. Tensor tuple is another access wrapper over those same aligned tensors.",
        "Use Shuffle training rows and its seed when a repeatable training order is appropriate. Configure reader processes, Prefetch and Timeout together: zero readers leaves Prefetch unset and Timeout zero. Collation must fit the model's actual representation; Fixed shapes combines equal shapes, while padded sequences and whole bags need their corresponding explicit input contracts. More reader processes are not automatically faster.",
        "In Metrics, compare Updates, Batches, Epoch and Time. Batches counts processed groups; Updates counts successful optimizer adjustments, so accumulation can make them differ. Raw shows observed interval loss; Smoothed changes only the Training curve. Pause chart freezes the display while work and capture continue. Drag the range controls to explore, then choose Fit. Reporting in the information popover changes the cadence for the next run; it cannot interrupt a batch to obtain a fresh measurement.",
        "Open Train’s Run settings. Model selection chooses which learned weights the trained-model output will use: Final epoch keeps the last completed pass; Best validation keeps the pass with the lowest measured Validation loss. A tie keeps the earlier pass. Early stopping is separate: enable it only when you want to stop after the chosen number of validation passes without an improvement larger than Minimum improvement. The Metrics outcome shows the selected pass and actual completed pass. Continuation retains the last optimizer and random state separately from the selected inference weights.",
        "In Settings → Training capture, enable Tensor heatmaps before a new run. Values remains the exact retained scalar sample; Heatmap summarizes declared regions using finite mean, minimum or maximum and reports partial coverage. Optional post-run playback retains up to the chosen frame count from existing validation passes. Choose Play or scrub the captured epochs; missing intervals are not invented. It stores bounded tile summaries, not every full tensor. Turning off run-output retention also turns off these optional captures for future runs. Older runs cannot acquire heatmaps afterward.",
        "Inspect the actual run and its saved settings in History. Changing access or batches changes the training policy; exact continuation must preserve the captured policy and row identity. Recorded Metrics uses the same chart when run-detail capture was enabled; older saved runs have epoch measurements only. Disabling capture skips optional within-epoch observations on disk, while required epoch metrics and model/replay evidence remain. Validation remains separate. Historical alignment and grouping assessments remain associated with their original saved lesson records. Opening this practice does not award those assessed completions."
    ]
    static let boundary = "Data access and Batches are configured on Train. Older saved workflow recipes retain their settings through the supported migration; historical scored lesson IDs and evidence remain readable. Training access still admits at most 100,000 prepared rows and 100 million tensor elements, within the other preparation, transport and memory budgets. It wraps dense prepared tensors; a finite stream or prefetch setting does not make model training out of core. Working data controls preparation sampling, whereas Train data access chooses observations from the prepared training partition. This editable practice has no scored pass, completion receipt or mastery award."
    static let legacyTitle = "Historical observations and grouping lessons"
    static let legacyExplanation = "Configure Train → Data access and Batches for current workflows. This historical link is retained so existing saved lessons and their assessment evidence remain readable. It does not offer new connected Dataset or DataLoader authoring. Open an existing lesson from Saved lessons to inspect its recorded work; the current Train practice below is editable and unscored."
}

struct TrainDataAccessLearningView: View {
    @ObservedObject var store: WorkspaceStore
    var body:some View {
        TrainingLessonReferenceView(entryID:"train-data-access") {
            Button("Open Train data access practice") {_ = store.loadComponentWorkflowExample(TrainDataAccessPracticeExample.exampleID,device:"cpu")}
                .buttonStyle(FlatButtonStyle()).disabled(store.isBusy).accessibilityIdentifier("learning.train-data-access.open-example")
        }
    }
}
